thinking:
Interesting: `builtin_for_index` only checks `index < g_builtin_count` (no `index <0` check in this master version!). And g_builtin_count = nitems(g_builtins), which includes the NULL sentinel entry appended at the end. So the sentinel is at index g_builtin_count-1, and builtin_for_index would return a pointer to the NULL-name sentinel for that last index - but builtin_getname then returns builtin->name == NULL, so the iteration in builtin_isavail terminates at the sentinel. Index0 is a real application entry (the sentinel is LAST).

Note the sentinel: entries are real apps at indices0..N-1, NULL sentinel at index N, g_builtin_count = N+1 (nitems counts all). builtin_isavail loops while builtin_getname(i) != NULL: for i in0..N-1 compares names, at i=N name is NULL -> loop ends. So a match at i=0 returns0. Great - index0 is a legal, real index.

Now I have everything. Summary of evidence:

1. lib_builtin_isavail.c (apache/nuttx master, libs/libc/builtin/):
```
for (i =0; (name = builtin_getname(i)) != NULL; i++)
 {
 if (strcmp(name, appname) ==0)
 {
 return i;
 }
 }

return -ENOENT;
```
Doc: "It returns a non-negative index to the application entry in the table of built-in applications on success or a negated errno value in the event of a failure." - Loop starts at i=0; first entry hit returns0. So0 is a legal return value (success, first table entry).

2. lib_builtin_getname.c: "index - From0 and on ..."; builtin_for_index(index); NULL if invalid.

3. builtin.h: builtin_isavail doc: "returns a non-negative index ... on success or a negated errno value in the event of a failure"; builtin_getname: "index, from0 and on ..."; builtin_for_index: "index, from0 and on...". Explicitly0-based.

4. Table generation:
 - nuttx/tools contains NO mkbuiltinlist script (list of mk* tools: mkallsyms, mkconfig, mkconfigvars, mkctags, mkdeps, mkexport, mkfsdata, mknulldeps, mkpasswd, mkromfsimg, mksymtab, mksyscall, mkversion, mkwindeps - no builtin-list generator).
 - nuttx root Makefile just includes tools/Unix.mk (or Win.mk) - no builtin_list rule in nuttx tree.
 - The table is generated in nuttx-apps: apps/builtin/builtin_list.c contains `const struct builtin_s g_builtins[] = { #include "builtin_list.h" ... { NULL,0,0,0 } };` - NULL sentinel appended AFTER all real entries (i.e., at the END, so index0 is a real app).
 - builtin_list.h is generated either:
 a) Makefile (legacy): from registry/*.bdat files: `BDATLIST = $(strip $(call RWILDCARD, registry, *.bdat))` concatenated (wildcard/glob order = filename order, which is <APPNAME>_main.bdat per app; no explicit name sort, order follows registration file set);
 b) CMake (current, used by this competition per build log): apps/builtin/CMakeLists.txt iterates `get_property(nuttx_app_libs GLOBAL PROPERTY NUTTX_APPS_LIBRARIES)` in the order apps were added (link/build order), appending `{ "name", prio, stack, main },` per module. No alphabetical sort.
 - Registration source: apps root Makefile `register_all: $(foreach SDIR, $(CONFIGURED_APPS), $(SDIR)_register)` - order of CONFIGURED_APPS.
 Conclusion: table order = registration/build order (not guaranteed alphabetical); index0 = first registered app = real entry; NULL sentinel is last.

5. binfmt/builtin.c:
```
index = builtin_isavail(filename);
if (index <0)
 {
 berr("ERROR: %s is not a builtin application\n", filename);
 return index;
 }
```
=> official kernel-side check: error iff index <0; index0 is treated as valid.

6. nsh_fileapps.c (nuttx-apps):
```
index = builtin_isavail(appname);
if (index >=0)
 { ... builtin_for_index(index); ... }
```
=> official apps-side check: success iff index >=0; index0 valid.

7. nshlib/Kconfig:
```
config NSH_BUILTIN_AS_COMMAND	bool "Run builtin apps as command"
	default ndepends on BUILTIN---help---
Enable to run builtin application directly without creating	a separate thread.
```
Note: the config name in current master is NSH_BUILTIN_AS_COMMAND (per the Kconfig text fetched). Good.

Bonus: lib_builtin_forindex.c: `if (index < g_builtin_count) return &g_builtins[index]; return NULL;` -0 is within bounds.

Note on g_builtin_count: `nitems(g_builtins)` counts the sentinel too. Minor detail worth mentioning: the loop in builtin_isavail terminates because sentinel name is NULL.

